{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-07): OPTION A — advance the baseline to what was ACTUALLY PUSHED.\n\nRationale: A restores what ADR 0026 itself defines the baseline to be — the LAST-SYNCED value — where the implementation currently stores the last-FETCHED value (`docs/adr/0026-*.md:22-42`). It fixes the defect for all five mirrored fields rather than status alone, and it preserves the inbound mirroring of genuine Jira-side edits that the ADR deliberately supports. Option B was rejected because it papers over the ADR rather than implementing it, and silently gives up a documented capability.\n\n**The constraint that makes A correct or dangerous** — this is the whole risk of the option and must be handled explicitly, not assumed: a status transition can SOFT-FAIL while the pass still exits 0 (`applier.py:725-726`). So the baseline must advance ONLY on per-mutation success. Advancing on 'the pass ran' would record a sync that never happened, which is strictly worse than today's bug: today a clobber is self-limiting (the baseline advances to Jira's value, local then differs, the next pass pushes local out), whereas a falsely-advanced baseline would make rebar believe local and Jira agree when they do not, and it would NOT self-correct.\n\nRequired, beyond the mechanical change:\n* Per-mutation success must be the gate. If the applier does not currently expose a per-field/per-mutation outcome the baseline advance can key off, that plumbing IS part of this ticket — do not approximate it with the pass exit code.\n* A RED oracle reproducing the observed ABA sequence: push a value to Jira, revert locally to the previous value within the same window, and assert the local revert SURVIVES the next pass. It must fail against current main.\n* A HELD-FIRM test that a genuine Jira-side status edit still flows inbound, so the fix does not quietly become option B by accident.\n* A soft-fail test: a transition that soft-fails must NOT advance the baseline.\n\n**Close the coverage gap while you are here.** ADR 0026 mandates a direction-preservation cell and it exists for `title` ONLY. The status equivalent (`test_inbound_field_sync_directionality.py::test_jira_side_status_change_suppressed`) drives `adapters/jira/outbound_fields._diff_fields`, which has NO PRODUCTION CALLER — status has never been pinned on the live path. A test that exercises a function nothing calls is the same class as 599e (an entire test surface passing by not running) and 24f7 (a tree-wide check that was a manual grep). Add the cell on the LIVE path for every mirrored field, not just the one that broke."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786127113385270001,"uuid":"0ef5a783-f729-45ad-aeb1-68a63c992b19"}